    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d179">

                <pb facs="0179" n="167" sameAs="frus:frus1941v01/pg167"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">701.4161/42: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in the Soviet Union</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Steinhardt</hi>) <hi
                        rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Moscow</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1941-06-09T18:00:00+03:00">June 9, 1941—6
                            p.m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received June 9—5:15 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>1117. My 1104, June 7, 3 p.m.<anchor n="30" xml:id="d179-f1"/> I have learned
                    from a source in which I have full confidence that the British Ambassador called
                    on Vyshinski shortly prior to his sudden departure<anchor n="31"
                        xml:id="d179-f2"/> and that when Vyshinski inquired as to the probable date
                    of Cripps’ return the latter sought to create the impression that his return was
                    in doubt.<anchor n="32" xml:id="d179-f3"/> My informant states that the Soviet
                    Foreign Office then telegraphed the Soviet Ambassador in London<anchor n="33"
                        xml:id="d179-f4"/> informing him of the conversation between Vyshinski and
                    Cripps and that Maisky thereupon called at the British Foreign Office in an
                    endeavor to ascertain whether Cripps was returning to Moscow and if not why
                    not.</p>
                <p>My informant stated that he believed the subsequent announcement by the British
                    Broadcasting Corporation that Cripps had been recalled to London for a
                    conference in the normal course of events was designed to quiet the Soviet
                    Government.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Steinhardt</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d179-f1" n="30" xml:id="d179-f1-content">
                <p>Not printed.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d179-f2" n="31" xml:id="d179-f2-content">
                <p>Sir Stafford Cripps left Moscow for Stockholm by airplane on the morning of June
                    6, 1941.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d179-f3" n="32" xml:id="d179-f3-content">
                <p>Cripps did return and was not replaced until February 1942.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d179-f4" n="33" xml:id="d179-f4-content">
                <p>Ivan Mikhailovich Maisky.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
